  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/4891c7eea304bff2598ba5b19118951183b196eb4f2f4249573706b6625066e2.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}={15}^{∘},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}={15}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{15}^{∘}={165}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}+{15}^{∘}={195}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{15}^{∘}={345}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, $\\theta $ can be equal to ${15}^{∘},$ ${165}^{∘},$ ${195}^{∘},$ and ${345}^{∘}.$",
